    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This dissertation contains a theoretical analysis of the mechanics of emulsification in porous media. The part played by the porous medium is a controversial question and has been largely overlooked by the members of the petroleum production industry. Much of the crude oil produced is in the form of stable emulsions. It is often claimed that these emulsions are formed as the oil and the water flow simultaneously through the chokes and other flow constrictions in oil field equipment. Even though this view is certainly reasonable, emulsions are still produced in wells which not only lack these constrictions but also produce at low flow rates. Also, in certain laboratory experiments, the concurrent flow of crude oil and brine results in the production of emulsions. This strongly suggests that at least in some cases the emulsions are produced in the reservoir rock itself. and hence, the need for a study of the role of the porous medium in emulsion formation.A mechanism is proposed whereby the disruption of the bulk interface between two liquids leads to the initial formation of the dispersed phase. This is based on the instability of the interface when a liquid of lower viscosity displaces one of a higher viscosity. Any disturbance of the interface is amplified and finally jets of one liquid shoot out into the other phase. Thereafter, other mechanisms in addition to the above, operate and result in the formation of droplets.The dispersion process has been studied by the application of perturbation methods to liquid interfaces. Criteria for the hydrodynamic stability of the various models proposed are developed. Critical conditions at which instability occurs are predicted. The number of droplets which are formed and their radius are then determined.There are two fundamental differences between this study and previous analyses. Although the problem of emulsification in porous media has not been investigated before, it has now been shown that emulsions can be formed within porous systems. This has been done with due care being paid to the viscometric aspects of the dispersion of one of the fluids into the other. The second and more important contribution is the hypothesis that viscosity differences between two liquids can lead to the formation of emulsions in porous media.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
